SPIKE RESULT (2026-09-05): the caller CANNOT pin a gateway tab id.

Evidence — src/launch_request_builder.go:205-206:
    id := b.newTabID(kind)
    session := b.deps.TmuxSession + "-" + id

terminalTabRequest (src/main.go:462-471) carries Kind, CWD, Session, Options, Isolate, WorktreeName — there is NO ID field, and decodeTerminalTabRequest (src/terminal_runtime_tabs.go:506) adds none. The id is server-minted unconditionally and the tmux session name is derived from it.

TRAP: req.Session exists on the request and is decoded, but the builder overwrites it at line 206. It is honoured only on the read-back paths (terminal_runtime_tabs.go:304, 367, 949). A caller supplying session in the POST body gets silently ignored.

OPTIONS, cheapest first:
1. Honour a caller-supplied req.Session in the builder when present, validated against the TmuxSession prefix. The field, the plumbing to startTTYDTab and ensureTmuxSession's has-session reuse (terminal_runtime_tabs.go:724) all already exist — this is roughly a three-line change and it turns the existing reuse path into a working adopt path. Recommended.
2. Add a dedicated adopt endpoint taking an existing session name + kind + cwd and writing a tab record around it. More surface, clearer contract.
3. Invert: let the gateway create the tab, read back its id -> session name, and have the topology layer attach to that session instead of creating its own. No gateway change at all, but the orchestrator no longer owns session creation.

NOT viable: the NewTabID dependency-injection seam (launch_request_builder.go:69-71) is in-process only and not reachable over HTTP.
